    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen`. It builds and `go vet` / `go test ./...` pass, but there are no test files and no database credentials, so none of the reopen behaviour has actually been run.\n\n**Changes (only `internal/candidate/` was edited):**\n- **`operations.go`**: declares `reopenOp` with operation `reopen.v5`, role requester, the requester's own purchases only, status rejected → submitted. It is a single-target operation, so the response is `{\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`. It writes the outbox event `purchase.reopened`, which `internal/notify` already sends to the tenant's approvers.\n- **`approval.go`**: a new `item` handler calls `executor.Run(ctx, u, reopenOp, key, []string{id}, reason)`. It converts `action.Rejection` to `protocol.Rejection` through the existing `adapt` and returns `protocol.ItemResult`. The route is registered with `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", …)`. Every other request passes through to the existing bulk-approve/bulk-reject routes and then the original server, so those APIs are unchanged.\n\n**How the requirements are met (from reading the code, not from running it):**\n- **Validation and auth**: 401/403, body checks, the size limit and Idempotency-Key checks are done by `protocol.Item`. Identity comes only from `AuthenticateDemo`, so client context headers can't override it.\n- **Atomic writes and 503**: the kernel writes the status change, the ApprovalAudit row (with the reason), the PurchaseEvent row and the ActionRequest row in one transaction. If the ActionRequest write fails, the whole transaction rolls back and the client gets 503, so the same key can be retried afterwards.\n- **Eligibility**: the kernel only selects rows with the current tenant, `createdBy` = current user and status `rejected`. RLS policy `purchase_transition` enforces the same condition. Anything else gets 409 with nothing written.\n- **Concurrency**: the same key is serialized by an advisory lock, so the second request replays the first. Different keys on the same purchase are serialized by `FOR UPDATE`; the loser sees status `submitted` and gets 409.\n\n**Known limit:** the shared request hash (`ledger.Fingerprint`) covers only the operation name and the purchase ID, not the reason. So reusing a key on the same purchase with a different reason is treated as a replay (200, `Idempotency-Replayed: true`), not 409. The spec only defines the same-reason case, so this isn't a violation. I left it this way because fixing it would need changes to protected code: the hash is checked by the nightly reconciliation (`ledger.Verify`), and requesters have no read access to ApprovalAudit where the reason is stored."
    ],
